  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1)\n\nMid level short wave passing from the Eastern Great Lakes into New\nEngland placing the Ohio Valley in Northwest flow. Surface high\npressure will offer dry conditions today with a much cooler airmass\nover the area. These cooler temperatures with highs mainly in the \n40s will be in place thru the week. \n\nThere is better agreement with energy dropping south from the\nNorthern Plains carving out a mid and upper level low that deepens as\nit develops into the Ohio Valley Thursday/Thursday night. \n\nPrecipitation will likely spread up into ILN/s area late Wednesday \nnight and into Thanksgiving day and then linger through Friday before\ntapering off Friday night. Pcpn should be be mainly rain but the \nmodels continue to trend cooler with the cold air aloft for late Thursday\nnight into Friday morning. A widespread 1 to 2 inch rainfall looks\nlikely from this evening. \n\nThe track of the upper level low could lead to a better chance of \nsome snow mixing in during the Thursday night/Friday morning period\nwhen the upper level low tracks across the area. Some light accumulations\nmainly on grassy areas is not out of the question but warm ground \ntemperatures should limit impacts. Will have to watch later runs and \npcpn rates as this can overcome warm ground temperatures and lead to \nminor impacts as was observed last November.",
